# EdminBoost Standards

Cross-cutting quality standards. See also: `edminboost-project.mdc` (architecture), `edminboost-compliance.mdc` (pre-ship gate), `edminboost-features.mdc` (modules), `edminboost-command-center.mdc` (Command Center), `edminboost-qa.mdc` (PHPUnit + Playwright), `wordpress-php.mdc` (PHP), `admin-assets.mdc` (JS/CSS).

## Coding Standards

- **PHP**: WordPress PHP Coding Standards — spacing, arrays, Yoda conditions only when required by the WPCS ruleset, docblocks where useful, clear naming.
- **JS**: WordPress-compatible admin JS patterns; avoid unnecessary frameworks unless justified.
- **CSS**: Scope styles to plugin UI; avoid bleeding into wp-admin or themes. Exceptions: visual theme (`edminboost-themes.css` styles `#wpadminbar` / `#adminmenu` when active); CC bar and drawer assets per `edminboost-command-center.mdc`.
- **HTML**: Semantic markup, accessible labels, buttons, headings; table markup only when appropriate.
- Never output inline CSS/JS unless there is a good reason.
- Keep lines and functions readable rather than clever.

## I18N

- All user-facing strings must be translatable.
- Use `EDMINBOOST_TEXT_DOMAIN` consistently; it must match `EDMINBOOST_PLUGIN_SLUG`.
- Use `__()`, `_e()`, `esc_html__()`, `esc_attr__()`, etc. appropriately.
- Feature `$name` and `$description` on `EDMINBOOST_Feature_Base` are translated via `get_name()` / `get_description()`.
- Text domain loads from `/languages` on `plugins_loaded` via `EDMINBOOST_I18n`.

## Accessibility

- Every settings field must have a label.
- Use `<button>` elements for actions, not clickable divs.
- Respect keyboard navigation and focus states.
- Use meaningful headings and notices.
- Provide screen-reader text where needed (e.g. `screen-reader-text` legends on fieldsets).

## Admin UX

- Add notices sparingly and only when relevant.
- Any notice must be dismissible or disappear when resolved.
- Do not place ads or upsells outside the plugin's own screens unless absolutely necessary and policy-safe.
- Avoid cluttering the admin menu.
- Load plugin-screen assets only on this plugin's pages; exceptions: `edminboost-themes` CSS on all wp-admin when the visual theme is active; `edminboost-command-center-bar` CSS/JS on Layout Studio (mapper drawer preview) and when the CC bar is active (admin bar showing); drawer shell when active or on mapper (admin + front end when admin bar shows).
- Features must respect the global `enabled` kill switch via `EDMINBOOST_Settings::is_enabled()`.
- Admin footer replacement is opt-in only (enabled + non-empty text).

## Performance

- Load code only where needed.
- Avoid expensive queries on every request.
- Cache remote calls and expensive computations.
- Avoid autoloading large options (`edminboost_settings` and `edminboost_version` are not autoloaded).
- Avoid front-end impact unless the plugin's purpose requires it (CC bar CSS/JS and visual theme CSS on `wp_enqueue_scripts` when the bar/theme is active are the current exceptions).

## Privacy

- No tracking, telemetry, or external data transmission.
- If the plugin ever sends data externally, explain what is sent, why, and require explicit opt-in.
- If personal data is stored, provide exporter/eraser integration when appropriate.

## Uninstall & Lifecycle

- **Deactivate**: no data removal (`EDMINBOOST_Deactivator` is a no-op; no cron to clear).
- **Delete**: `uninstall.php` removes `edminboost_settings` and `edminboost_version`.
- State data retention clearly in `readme.txt`.
- If adding new options, update `uninstall.php` accordingly.

## Release

- Include `readme.txt` structure suitable for WordPress.org if requested.
- Include license headers.
- Include versioning (`EDMINBOOST_VERSION` + plugin header).
- Ensure the plugin is complete and stable enough for review.
